{{Main|2016 United States Senate election in Alaska}}
{{See also|List of United States senators from Alaska}}
Two-term senator [[Lisa Murkowski]] (Republican) was appointed in 2002 and elected to a full term in 2004. She was defeated in the Republican primary in 2010 by [[Joe Miller (Alaska politician)|Joe Miller]]. She later ran as a write-in candidate in the 2010 general election and was re-elected to a second full term with 40% of the vote, making her one of two senators in US history to win election via write-in votes. She was 59 years old in 2016. She ran for re-election.<ref name=lisaannounces/>

Thomas Lamb, a candidate for the state House in [[2006 Alaska state elections|2006]], and Bob Lochner filed to run against Murkowski.<ref name=TLamb>{{cite web | title=August 16, 2016 Primary Candidate List | url=http://www.elections.alaska.gov/election/2016/Primary/candidate_info_prm_2016.php | publisher=State of Alaska Division of Elections | access-date=March 6, 2016 | archive-date=June 29, 2016 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160629003709/http://www.elections.alaska.gov/election/2016/Primary/candidate_info_prm_2016.php | url-status=dead }}</ref> Other potential Republican primary challengers included 2010 nominee and 2014 candidate [[Joe Miller (Alaska politician)|Joe Miller]], State Senator [[Mike J. Dunleavy]], former lieutenant governor [[Mead Treadwell]], and former mayor of [[Anchorage, Alaska|Anchorage]] [[Dan Sullivan (Mayor of Anchorage)|Dan Sullivan]].<ref>{{cite news | last=Pathe | first=Simone | date=May 15, 2015 | title=Murkowski Facing a Primary Puzzle | url=http://atr.rollcall.com/lisa-murkowski-primary-joe-miller-mike-dunleavy | work=[[Roll Call]] | access-date=December 23, 2015 | archive-date=December 15, 2015 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151215093250/http://atr.rollcall.com/lisa-murkowski-primary-joe-miller-mike-dunleavy/ | url-status=dead }}</ref>

The only person to file for the Democratic primary as of May 20 was writer and satirist [[Richard Grayson (writer)|Richard Grayson]], who previously sought election to Wyoming's House seat in 2014.<ref>{{cite news | last1=Neary | first1=Ben | title=The Only Democrat Running For Congress In Wyoming Might Host A 'Campaign Rave' | url=http://trib.com/news/state-and-regional/govt-and-politics/arizona-man-the-only-democratic-challenger-to-wyoming-rep-cynthia/article_8de2bdff-f5d1-5104-9206-220016a37619.html | access-date=May 20, 2016 | agency=Associated Press | date=June 13, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news | last1=Lawrence | first1=Tom | title=EDITORIAL: Lessons we learned from Election Day | url=http://www.powelltribune.com/opinion/editorials/item/13047-editorial-lessons-we-learned-from-election-day | access-date=May 20, 2016 | publisher=Powell Tribune | date=November 6, 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite news | last1=John | first1=Arit | title=Wyoming's Democratic 'Hip Hop Candidate' Just Launched the PPLZ 4 GRAYSON CREW | url=http://www.thewire.com/politics/2014/06/wyomings-democratic-hip-hop-candidate-just-launched-the-pplz-4-grayson-crew/372767 | access-date=May 20, 2016 | agency=The Wire | date=June 13, 2014 | archive-date=June 17, 2016 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160617055723/http://www.thewire.com/politics/2014/06/wyomings-democratic-hip-hop-candidate-just-launched-the-pplz-4-grayson-crew/372767/ | url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name=TLamb/> Potential Democratic candidates included State Senator [[Dennis Egan]], State Representative [[Andy Josephson]], State Senator [[Bill Wielechowski]], State Senator [[Hollis French]] and State Senate Minority Leader [[Johnny Ellis]].<ref name="Alaska Farm Team">{{cite news | last=Trygstad | first=Kyle | url=http://www.rollcall.com/news/farm_team_absence_of_opportunity_means_long_alaska_pipeline-222421-1.html?pg=2 | title=Farm Team: Absence of Opportunity Means Long Alaska Pipeline | access-date=December 12, 2013 | publisher=[[Roll Call]] | date=February 13, 2013 | archive-date=September 24, 2015 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924123947/http://www.rollcall.com/news/farm_team_absence_of_opportunity_means_long_alaska_pipeline-222421-1.html?pg=2 | url-status=dead }}</ref> Former senator [[Mark Begich]] was mentioned as a possible candidate,<ref>{{cite news | last=Sullivan | first=Sean| date=February 2, 2015 | newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] | url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/post-politics/wp/2015/02/02/why-mark-begich-could-run-for-the-senate-again | title=Why Mark Begich could run for the Senate again| access-date=February 16, 2015}}</ref> but he declined to run.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.adn.com/article/20160216/former-sen-begich-says-he-wont-run-any-office-year | last=Bohrer| first=Becky | date=February 16, 2016 | title=Begich says he won't run for any office this year | work=[[Alaska Dispatch News]]| access-date=February 18, 2016}}</ref>

Murkowski won her primary on August 16, 2016 with 72 percent of the vote. Joe Miller received the Libertarian nomination and ran against Murkowski in the general election. Anchorage attorney and veteran [[Margaret Stock]] ran as an Independent candidate.<ref name=Stock>{{cite news | last=Kelly | first=Devin | date=February 17, 2016 | title=Anchorage attorney Margaret Stock challenging Lisa Murkowski for Senate | url=http://www.adn.com/article/20160217/anchorage-attorney-margaret-stock-challenging-lisa-murkowski-senate | newspaper=Alaska Dispatch News | access-date=February 19, 2016}}</ref>

Murkowski won re-election with 44% of the vote, compared to Miller with 30% and Metcalfe with 11%. 15% went to other candidates. Murkowski has been re-elected three times now with 48% in 2004, 39.5% in 2010 and 44% in 2016, never having won a majority.

